  "worked_solution": "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}x \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
